Chapter: Deriving Eq Recursive
  cites Foreword chapter Console

Section: Types

 A RECURSIVE sum that also carries `deriving Eq`. Nothing else in the tree
 spells that combination: `typeclass-smoke` derives Eq on a non-recursive
 `Wrap` and on a record, and `recursive-eq` exercises a recursive sum with
 no deriving clause at all. Two mechanisms name the same definition for
 this shape -- the desugarer's `deriving` path and the x86-64 emitter's
 recursive helper are both `__eq_` followed by the type name -- and until
 this chapter existed no program in the corpus asked what happens when
 both apply to one type.

  Tree =
    | Leaf
    | Fork (Tree) (Tree)
    deriving Eq

Section: Builders

 Built through functions rather than written as literals at each use, so
 neither arm can fold two constructions into one object. A POINTER compare
 answers the different-shape and self-comparison rows correctly, so a
 chapter made only of equal-shape rows could not tell an identity compare
 from a structural one and would pass while proving nothing.

  leaf : Tree
  leaf = Leaf

  mk-fork : Tree, Tree -> Tree
  mk-fork (l) (r) = Fork l r

  pair-tree : Tree
  pair-tree = mk-fork leaf leaf

  nested-tree : Tree
  nested-tree = mk-fork (mk-fork leaf leaf) leaf

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line (if pair-tree == mk-fork leaf leaf then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if pair-tree == leaf then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if leaf == leaf then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if nested-tree == mk-fork (mk-fork leaf leaf) leaf then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if nested-tree == pair-tree then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if pair-tree == pair-tree then "eq" else "ne")
  end

